            <ArticleTitle>This Is My (Post) Truth, Tell Me Yours Comment on &quot;The Rise of Post-truth Populism in Pluralist Liberal Democracies: Challenges for Health Policy&quot;.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>723-725</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.15171/ijhpm.2017.58</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This is a commentary on the article 'The rise of post-truth populism in pluralist liberal democracies: challenges for health policy.' It critically examines two of its key concepts: populism and 'post truth.' This commentary argues that there are different types of populism, with unclear links to impacts, and that in some ways, 'post-truth' has resonances with arguments advanced in the period at the beginning of the British National Health Service (NHS). In short, 'post-truth' populism' may be 'déjà vu all over again,' and there are multiple (post) truths: this is my (post) truth, tell me yours.</AbstractText>
